n React, Hooks are special functions that let functional components use React features such as state, lifecycle behavior, context, and references.

Before Hooks, these features often required class components. Hooks were introduced in React 16.8.

Example: useState
import { useState } from "react";

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>

      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Increase
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

Here:

const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
useState → a React Hook
count → current state value
setCount → function used to update it
0 → initial value

When setCount() changes the state, React re-renders the component with the new value.

Common React Hooks
Hook	Purpose
useState	Store and update state
useEffect	Run side effects, such as API calls
useContext	Access context/shared data
useRef	Store a reference/value without causing re-renders
useMemo	Cache a calculated value
useCallback	Cache a function
Custom Hooks	Reuse your own stateful logic

For example, useEffect can run code when a component loads:

import { useEffect } from "react";

function App() {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Component mounted");
  }, []);

  return <h1>Hello</h1>;
}

A simple way to remember it is:

Component = UI
Hooks = extra React functionality used inside the component

Two important rules are that Hooks should generally be called at the top level of a React component (not inside loops or conditions), and only from React components or custom Hooks.


