📚 DAY 22 — useEffect
1. What is useEffect?

useEffect is a React Hook used when your component needs to perform something outside of simply calculating and displaying JSX.

Basic syntax:

useEffect(() => {
  // side effect code
});

Example:

import { useEffect } from "react";

function App() {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Component rendered");
  });

  return <h1>Hello React</h1>;
}

The important word is:

Effect

An effect is something your component does because of rendering, rather than something that directly produces JSX.

2. Why do we need useEffect?

Imagine your React application needs to:

Fetch data from an API
Start a timer
Subscribe to an event
Listen for window resize
Change the document title
Connect to a WebSocket
Read/write something external to React

These are called side effects.

For example:

React component
      │
      ├── Calculate UI
      │
      └── Interact with outside world
              │
              ├── API
              ├── Browser
              ├── Timer
              ├── WebSocket
              └── Event listener

useEffect gives React a controlled place to handle these operations.

3. Real-World Analogy 🏠

Imagine you own a restaurant.

Your React component is the restaurant.

Rendering the menu is normal work:

Restaurant
   ↓
Display menu

But suppose you also need to:

Call the supplier
Start the kitchen timer
Listen for a delivery
Update an external display

These are activities happening outside the menu itself.

That's similar to a React side effect.

React Component
       ↓
Render UI
       │
       └──── useEffect
                ↓
          Outside activity
4. Syntax

The basic syntax is:

useEffect(() => {
  // effect
}, []);

There are three important parts:

useEffect(
  () => {
    // effect
  },
  []
);
Part 1
useEffect

React's Hook.

Part 2
() => {
   // effect
}

A function containing the side-effect code.

Part 3
[]

The dependency array.

This controls when the effect should run.

5. Line-by-Line Explanation

Consider:

import { useEffect } from "react";

function App() {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Hello");
  }, []);

  return <h1>Hello React</h1>;
}

export default App;

Let's understand every line.

Line 1
