⭐ The Most Important Concept: Dependency Array

There are three important forms.

Case 1 — No dependency array
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Effect");
});

This runs after every render.

Render
 ↓
Effect

Render
 ↓
Effect

Render
 ↓
Effect

Be careful with this.

Case 2 — Empty dependency array
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Effect");
}, []);

This runs after the component mounts.

Conceptually:

Component appears
       ↓
Effect runs

It does not re-run simply because the component renders again.

Case 3 — Dependencies
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Count changed");
}, [count]);

Now React watches count.

count changes
     ↓
React renders
     ↓
Effect runs

Example:

const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Count:", count);
}, [count]);

Whenever count changes, the effect runs.

🧠 Think of Dependencies Like a Security Camera

Suppose:

useEffect(() => {
  console.log("User changed");
}, [user]);

You are telling React:

"Watch user. If it changes, run this effect."

          user
           ↓
       👀 WATCH
           ↓
      Did it change?
       /          \
     No            Yes
     ↓              ↓
Nothing          Run effect
7. Beginner Example

Let's create a simple document title updater.

import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = `Count: ${count}`;
  }, [count]);

  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
      Count: {count}
    </button>
  );
}

export default App;
What happens?

Initially:

count = 0

Browser title:

Count: 0

Click button.

count = 1

Effect runs.

Browser title becomes:

Count: 1

Again:

count = 2

Title:

Count: 2
🔥 8. Real-World Example — API Call

One of the most common uses of useEffect is fetching data.

Imagine an e-commerce website.

Product Page
     ↓
Component renders
     ↓
useEffect
     ↓
API request
     ↓
Server
     ↓
Product data
     ↓
setProducts()
     ↓
React re-renders
     ↓
Products displayed

Example:

import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

function Products() {
  const [products, setProducts] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function fetchProducts() {
      const response = await fetch(
        "https://fakestoreapi.com/products"
      );

      const data = await response.json();

      setProducts(data);
    }

    fetchProducts();
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      {products.map((product) => (
        <p key={product.id}>
          {product.title}
        </p>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

export default Products;
