      ↓
Cleanup runs
      ↓
Timer stops

Without cleanup:

Component disappears
       ↓
Timer continues ❌
       ↓
Memory/resource waste

With cleanup:

Component disappears
       ↓
Cleanup
       ↓
Timer stops ✅
🎯 Event Listener Example

Bad:

useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener("resize", handleResize);
}, []);

We added an event listener but never removed it.

Better:

useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener("resize", handleResize);

  return () => {
    window.removeEventListener("resize", handleResize);
  };
}, []);

The cleanup reverses what the effect did.

Think:

Setup
 ↓
addEventListener()

Cleanup
 ↓
removeEventListener()
🚨 10. Common Mistakes
Mistake 1 — Infinite loop

Consider:

const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

useEffect(() => {
  setCount(count + 1);
});

What's wrong?

There is no dependency array.

Render
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
setCount()
 ↓
Render
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
setCount()
 ↓
Render
 ↓
∞

This can cause an infinite render loop.

Mistake 2 — Missing dependencies

Example:

useEffect(() => {
  console.log(user.name);
}, []);

If the effect depends on user, but user isn't included, you can get stale values.

Usually:

useEffect(() => {
  console.log(user.name);
}, [user]);
Mistake 3 — Forgetting cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener("resize", handleResize);
}, []);

Add cleanup:

useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener("resize", handleResize);

  return () => {
    window.removeEventListener("resize", handleResize);
  };
}, []);
Mistake 4 — Using useEffect for everything

This is a very important industry lesson.

Don't automatically use:

useEffect()

whenever something happens.

For example, if you want to calculate:

const total = price * quantity;

you usually don't need:

useEffect(() => {
  setTotal(price * quantity);
}, [price, quantity]);

Instead:

const total = price * quantity;

Why?

Because total can be calculated directly during rendering.

Rule

Use useEffect to synchronize with external systems, not merely to calculate values.

This is a very important modern React principle.

